#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180354} :activity/id #uuid "48809555-7500-5dc1-824d-52a5a22edeba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580 :topic/id #uuid "557f8b58-be49-555e-8162-3d1448eaff0c" :topic/math-academy-id 3728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180785 :tutorial/content "The range of a function can be thought of as the set of $y$-values given by the function's graph. For that reason, it's often helpful to plot the function when determining its range.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) =3-2x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any real number. What is the range of $f(x)$?\n\nSince this is a linear function, we only need two points to plot it. For example:\n\n- When $x=0$, we have $y=3$. This gives the point $(0,3)$.\n- When $x=1$, we have $y=1$. This gives the point $(1,1)$.\n\nPlotting these two points and connecting them, we obtain the following diagram.\n\n![](images/51/5124e487118191ae40248d4488450d065fe71712f709ac62321d749ac17b0127.png)\n\nNotice that the graph $y=f(x)$ will cover all possible $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty < y < \\infty\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $(-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example of a linear function with a restricted domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a33428f-024c-5783-b5d1-b4b0f342d4a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8800 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b396314a-88fd-58e7-a7b9-40fb4292040b" :step/math-academy-id 192940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :question/id #uuid "dc5cefa9-aa18-52d3-bbdc-a0c1050f6f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14168"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049